-- Q1: medium / L3 — Noncash investing/financing supplemental schedule disclosure
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Hargrove Corp. converts $2 million in bonds payable into 100,000 shares of common stock during 2025. The CFO prepares the statement of cash flows. How should this conversion be reported?', '["As a financing activity inflow offset by a financing activity outflow", "As an investing activity outflow and a financing activity inflow", "In a supplemental schedule of noncash investing and financing activities", "As a reduction to cash from operating activities"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 230-10-50-3, noncash investing and financing activities must be disclosed in a supplemental schedule, not in the body of the statement of cash flows. The debt-to-equity conversion involves no cash exchange and therefore does not appear in any of the three activity categories. Option A is incorrect because the transaction does not involve cash flows, even though both elements relate to financing.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Cash equivalents maximum original maturity
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Under U.S. GAAP, what is the maximum original maturity for an investment to qualify as a cash equivalent?', '["30 days", "60 days", "Three months", "Six months"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 230-10-20, cash equivalents are short-term, highly liquid investments readily convertible to known amounts of cash with original maturities of three months or less from the date of purchase. The standard emphasizes original maturity, not remaining maturity at the balance sheet date. Option D is incorrect because six months exceeds the threshold; only investments with original maturities of three months or less qualify.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Dividends paid vs dividends received CF classification
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Redfield Inc. paid $150,000 in dividends to its shareholders and received $40,000 in dividends from an equity investment during 2025. Under U.S. GAAP, how should these items be classified on the statement of cash flows?', '["Both classified as financing activities", "Dividends paid as financing; dividends received as operating", "Both classified as operating activities", "Dividends paid as operating; dividends received as investing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-15 and 230-10-45-28, dividends paid to shareholders are financing activities because they represent returns to owners. Dividends received from investments are operating activities because they represent returns on investments analogous to interest income. Option A is incorrect because dividends received are not financing activities under U.S. GAAP; they relate to operating cash flows.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Working capital definition
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'In the context of balance sheet analysis, what does working capital represent?', '["Total assets minus total liabilities", "Net income plus depreciation expense", "Cash and cash equivalents minus short-term debt", "Current assets minus current liabilities"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 210-10-45, working capital is defined as current assets minus current liabilities and measures an entity''s short-term liquidity position. It indicates whether the entity has sufficient short-term resources to cover near-term obligations. Option A is incorrect because total assets minus total liabilities equals total stockholders'' equity under the accounting equation, not working capital.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Indirect method operating CF computation with multiple adjustments
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Thornton Industries reports net income of $320,000 for 2025. During the year, depreciation expense was $45,000, amortization of a patent was $12,000, and accounts payable decreased by $18,000. Using the indirect method, what is cash provided by operating activities?', '["$377,000", "$359,000", "$395,000", "$341,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 230-10-45, the indirect method starts with net income and adjusts for noncash items and working capital changes. Net income $320,000 + depreciation $45,000 + amortization $12,000 − decrease in accounts payable $18,000 = $359,000. A decrease in accounts payable means cash was used to pay suppliers, reducing operating cash flow. Option A ($377,000) incorrectly adds the accounts payable decrease rather than subtracting it.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: easy / L2 — Treasury stock presentation as contra equity
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Greystone Corp. purchases 10,000 shares of its own common stock for $25 per share. How is this treasury stock reported on the balance sheet?', '["As a current asset at cost", "As a reduction of total stockholders'' equity", "As a long-term investment in the noncurrent assets section", "As additional paid-in capital"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 505-30, treasury stock is reported as a contra equity item, reducing total stockholders'' equity. Whether recorded using the cost method or par value method, treasury stock decreases equity and is not an asset. Option A is incorrect because a company''s own shares do not meet the definition of an asset; they represent a return of capital to selling shareholders.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Discontinued operations presentation location
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Kessler Enterprises disposes of a major product line that qualifies as a component of the entity under ASC 205-20. The segment had an operating loss of $200,000 and a disposal gain of $50,000, both net of tax. Where are these amounts reported on the income statement?', '["Combined as a $150,000 net loss within operating expenses", "In other comprehensive income", "As a prior period adjustment in retained earnings", "As separate line items below income from continuing operations"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 205-20-45-3, results of discontinued operations are reported net of tax as a separate component below income from continuing operations. Both the operating loss and disposal gain are presented within the discontinued operations section, not commingled with continuing operations. Option A is incorrect because discontinued operations must be segregated from operating expenses to enable users to assess ongoing earnings quality.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Direct method reports gross receipts and payments
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'What does the direct method of presenting operating cash flows report?', '["Gross cash receipts and gross cash payments from operations", "Net income adjusted for noncash items and working capital changes", "Only investing and financing cash flows", "Changes in each balance sheet account"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-25, the direct method presents operating cash flows by reporting major classes of gross cash receipts (e.g., cash from customers) and gross cash payments (e.g., cash paid to suppliers and employees). This shows actual cash flows rather than adjustments to net income. Option B is incorrect because it describes the indirect method, which reconciles net income to operating cash flows through noncash and working capital adjustments.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Equipment sale proceeds classified as investing inflow
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Mercer Holdings sells a piece of manufacturing equipment for $175,000 cash, recording a $30,000 loss on disposal. On the statement of cash flows, how should the $175,000 proceeds be classified?', '["Operating activity — cash inflow of $175,000", "Financing activity — cash inflow of $175,000", "Investing activity — cash inflow of $145,000", "Investing activity — cash inflow of $175,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-12, proceeds from the sale of long-term assets such as equipment are classified as investing activities. The full $175,000 cash received is reported as an investing inflow; the $30,000 loss is a noncash reconciling item under the indirect method. Option C ($145,000) is incorrect because the cash flow statement reports actual cash received, not the net book value or gain/loss-adjusted amount.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Bond issuance and stock repurchase both classified as financing
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Oakmont Corp. issues $5 million in 10-year bonds at par value during 2025 and uses part of the proceeds to repurchase $1.2 million of its own common stock. How are these transactions classified on the statement of cash flows?', '["Bond issuance as investing inflow; stock repurchase as operating outflow", "Bond issuance as operating inflow; stock repurchase as financing outflow", "Both classified as financing activities", "Bond issuance as financing inflow; stock repurchase as investing outflow"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-14 and 230-10-45-15, both transactions with creditors (issuing bonds) and transactions with owners (repurchasing stock) are classified as financing activities. The bond issuance is a $5 million financing inflow, and the stock repurchase is a $1.2 million financing outflow. Option A is incorrect because neither borrowing from creditors nor returning capital to shareholders involves investing or operating activities.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — AOCI reported in stockholders' equity section
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'In which section of the balance sheet is accumulated other comprehensive income (AOCI) reported?', '["Current assets", "Noncurrent liabilities", "Stockholders'' equity", "Other revenues and gains"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 220-10-45, accumulated other comprehensive income is reported as a separate component of stockholders'' equity on the balance sheet. AOCI represents the cumulative total of items that bypassed net income but affected equity, such as unrealized gains on AFS securities and foreign currency translation adjustments. Option A is incorrect because AOCI is not an asset; it is an equity adjustment from OCI items.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Income statement ordering: continuing, discontinued, OCI
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Bellingham Corp. has income from continuing operations of $800,000, a discontinued operations loss of $120,000 (net of tax), and other comprehensive income of $45,000. The CEO asks the controller about proper ordering. Which sequence correctly reflects U.S. GAAP presentation?', '["Discontinued operations, then continuing operations, then OCI", "Continuing operations, then discontinued operations, then OCI", "Continuing operations, then OCI, then discontinued operations", "OCI, then continuing operations, then discontinued operations"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 220-10-45 and ASC 205-20-45, the income statement presents income from continuing operations first, followed by discontinued operations (net of tax) to arrive at net income. OCI is presented either in a separate statement or below net income in a single continuous statement. Option A is incorrect because discontinued operations always follow continuing operations to preserve the distinction between ongoing and nonrecurring results.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — IFRS vs GAAP interest paid classification flexibility
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Eurotech S.A. prepares financial statements under IFRS and reports interest paid as a financing activity and dividends paid as a financing activity on its statement of cash flows. A U.S.-based analyst compares Eurotech''s cash flows with a GAAP-reporting peer. Which IFRS classification choice would NOT be permitted under U.S. GAAP, and what is the analytical consequence?', '["Classifying interest paid as financing — under GAAP it must be operating, which would decrease GAAP operating cash flows relative to IFRS", "Classifying dividends paid as financing — this is identical under GAAP, so no analytical difference arises", "Both classifications are permitted under GAAP, so the analyst needs no adjustment", "Classifying interest paid as financing — under GAAP it must also be financing, so no analytical difference arises"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IAS 7.33, IFRS allows interest paid to be classified as either operating or financing. Under ASC 230-10-45-17, U.S. GAAP requires interest paid as operating. If Eurotech classifies interest paid as financing, a GAAP peer would include it in operating, making the GAAP entity''s operating cash flows appear lower by comparison. Option D is incorrect because GAAP mandates interest paid as operating, not financing; the IFRS flexibility to classify interest paid outside operations creates a comparability difference.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Notes to financial statements are an integral part
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Under U.S. GAAP, what is the role of notes (footnotes) to the financial statements?', '["They replace the need for supplementary schedules", "They are optional disclosures provided at management''s discretion", "They are an integral part of the financial statements providing required disclosures", "They are only required for publicly traded companies"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 235-10-05, notes to the financial statements are an integral part of a complete set of financial statements. They provide disclosures required by GAAP, including accounting policies, disaggregation of line items, contingencies, and commitments. Option B is incorrect because notes are mandatory, not discretionary; omitting required disclosures results in a departure from GAAP that may affect the audit opinion.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — SPF missing basis-of-accounting note
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Crawford & Associates prepares its financial statements using the income tax basis of accounting. The firm''s accountant drafts the statements without any note disclosing the basis of accounting. Under AICPA standards for special purpose frameworks, what is the most significant reporting deficiency?', '["Failure to include a statement of cash flows", "Failure to disclose the basis of accounting and how it differs from GAAP", "Failure to present comparative financial statements", "Failure to include earnings per share data"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AICPA standards and ASC 105-10-05, financial statements prepared using a special purpose framework must include a note disclosing the basis of accounting used and a summary of significant differences from GAAP. Without this disclosure, users cannot assess how the statements differ from GAAP-basis reporting. Option A is incorrect because while a cash flow statement may be required, the omitted basis-of-accounting note is the more fundamental deficiency for SPF statements.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: hard / L4 — AFS reclassification from AOCI to net income; comprehensive income unaffected
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'During 2025, Palmer Corp. sells available-for-sale debt securities that had an unrealized gain of $85,000 in AOCI. The securities are sold at a gain of $85,000 recognized in net income. The CFO observes that comprehensive income should be unaffected by this reclassification. Evaluate whether the CFO''s observation is correct and identify the effects on net income and OCI.', '["Correct — net income increases by $85,000 and OCI decreases by $85,000, leaving comprehensive income unchanged", "Incorrect — comprehensive income increases by $85,000 because the gain is counted in both net income and OCI", "Incorrect — comprehensive income decreases by $85,000 because the reclassification removes the gain from equity entirely", "Correct — net income and OCI are both unchanged because the gain was already recognized when the securities were revalued"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 320-10-35 and ASC 220-10-45, when AFS securities are sold, the previously unrealized gain in AOCI is reclassified into net income via a reclassification adjustment. Net income increases by $85,000 and OCI decreases by the same amount, so comprehensive income (net income + OCI) is unchanged, preventing double-counting. Option B is incorrect because the reclassification adjustment simultaneously removes the $85,000 from OCI.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Credit risk on FV option liabilities never reclassified (PUFER "R")
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Under ASC 825-10, Hartwell Corp. elects the fair value option for a long-term liability. The liability''s fair value decreases by $200,000 in 2025 — $60,000 from changes in its own credit risk and $140,000 from benchmark interest rate changes. Will the credit risk component ever be reclassified from OCI to net income under the PUFER framework?', '["No — credit risk changes on fair value option liabilities are permanently reported in OCI and never reclassified to net income", "Yes — the credit risk component is reclassified to net income when the liability is settled or extinguished", "Yes — the credit risk component is reclassified to net income at the end of each reporting period", "No — the entire $200,000 fair value change is reported in net income, not OCI"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 825-10-45-5, changes in fair value of liabilities under the fair value option attributable to instrument-specific credit risk are reported in OCI and never reclassified to net income. This is the "R" in the PUFER mnemonic — Risk (credit) changes on FV option liabilities. The remaining $140,000 from interest rate changes goes to net income. Option B is incorrect because ASC 825 specifically prohibits reclassification of the credit risk component, even upon settlement.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Extraordinary items eliminated under ASU 2015-01
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Under current U.S. GAAP, how are extraordinary items reported on the income statement?', '["Net of tax, below discontinued operations", "As a separate line item within operating income", "They are no longer a valid classification and are not separately reported", "In other comprehensive income"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 225-20 as amended by ASU 2015-01, the concept of extraordinary items was eliminated from U.S. GAAP. Events previously classified as extraordinary (unusual and infrequent) are now reported within income from continuing operations without separate net-of-tax presentation. Option A is incorrect because the extraordinary items classification was removed entirely; no items receive this distinct treatment under current standards.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — Statement of stockholders' equity purpose
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Ashford Industries presents a statement of stockholders'' equity for 2025 showing beginning balances of common stock, APIC, retained earnings, treasury stock, and AOCI. Which of the following best describes the primary purpose of this statement?', '["To report cash flows from transactions with owners", "To reconcile the beginning and ending balances of each equity component", "To disclose off-balance-sheet financing arrangements", "To present a detailed breakdown of operating expenses"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 505-10-S99, the statement of stockholders'' equity reconciles the beginning and ending balance of each equity component by detailing transactions that caused changes during the period, such as net income, dividends, share issuances, and OCI. This ties the income statement to the balance sheet through retained earnings and AOCI. Option A is incorrect because while some owner transactions appear, the statement''s purpose is comprehensive equity reconciliation, not cash flow reporting.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Working capital changes net adjustment on indirect method
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Canton Manufacturing reports net income of $500,000 for 2025. Accounts receivable decreased by $35,000, inventory increased by $20,000, and accrued liabilities increased by $15,000. Using the indirect method, what is the net adjustment to net income for these working capital changes?', '["Add $30,000", "Subtract $30,000", "Add $70,000", "Subtract $40,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 230-10-45, the indirect method adjusts net income for working capital changes: a decrease in AR is added ($35,000), an increase in inventory is subtracted ($20,000), and an increase in accrued liabilities is added ($15,000). Net adjustment: $35,000 − $20,000 + $15,000 = +$30,000. Option B is incorrect because it reverses the signs; a decrease in receivables means more cash was collected than revenue recognized, increasing operating cash flows.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: hard / L3 — Restricted cash included in CF totals under ASU 2016-18
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Vanguard Corp. holds $2.5 million in unrestricted cash and $800,000 in restricted cash required as collateral for a long-term loan. At the beginning of 2025, the restricted cash balance was $500,000. Under ASU 2016-18, how should Vanguard present restricted cash on the statement of cash flows?', '["Restricted cash is excluded from cash and cash equivalents; the $300,000 increase is an investing outflow", "Restricted cash is included in the ending cash balance, and a reconciliation of all cash components is required", "Restricted cash is reported as a financing activity outflow of $800,000", "Restricted cash is disclosed only in the notes with no effect on the cash flow statement presentation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASU 2016-18 (codified in ASC 230-10-50-8), restricted cash and restricted cash equivalents must be included in the total cash balance on the statement of cash flows. Entities must provide a reconciliation of all cash, cash equivalents, restricted cash, and restricted cash equivalents on the face of the statement or in notes. Option A is incorrect because it reflects the pre-ASU treatment; restricted cash must now be included in the ending balance.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — Comparative statements: opening RE adjustment for error in non-presented period
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Stratton Corp. presents two-year comparative financial statements for 2025 and 2024. In March 2026, it discovers that $150,000 of revenue was recorded in 2023 that should have been recognized in 2024. The 2023 statements are not presented. The controller argues no restatement is needed because 2023 is not included. Evaluate this position.', '["The controller is correct — only presented periods require restatement, and 2023 is not presented", "The entire $150,000 must be recognized as 2025 revenue since the error period is not presented", "The error is immaterial because revenue was eventually recognized in a nearby period", "The controller is incorrect — the 2024 opening retained earnings must be adjusted because the error originated in a prior period and affects the 2024 beginning balance"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-5, when an error affects a period prior to the earliest presented, the opening balances of assets, liabilities, and retained earnings for the earliest period presented (2024) must be restated. Even though 2023 is not shown, the misstatement caused 2024''s opening retained earnings to be understated. Option A is incorrect because error correction extends to the cumulative effect on beginning balances of the earliest period presented.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Prepaid expenses classified as current asset
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'How are prepaid expenses classified on a classified balance sheet?', '["Noncurrent assets", "Current liabilities", "Stockholders'' equity", "Current assets"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 210-10-45-1, prepaid expenses are classified as current assets because they represent amounts paid in advance for goods or services expected to be consumed within one year or the operating cycle, whichever is longer. Examples include prepaid rent, insurance, and advertising. Option A is incorrect because prepaid expenses are typically consumed within the current operating period, meeting the definition of current rather than noncurrent assets.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Complex equity statement ending balance computation
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Meridian Corp. begins 2025 with common stock of $500,000, APIC of $4.5 million, retained earnings of $3.2 million, and AOCI of ($60,000). During 2025, the company records net income of $600,000, OCI of $45,000, declares cash dividends of $100,000, and issues 20,000 shares at $28 per share ($1 par). Evaluate the ending total stockholders'' equity.', '["$9,245,000", "$9,200,000", "$8,685,000", "$9,345,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 505-10-50, the statement of stockholders'' equity tracks all equity changes. Beginning equity: $500,000 + $4,500,000 + $3,200,000 − $60,000 = $8,140,000. Add net income ($600,000), OCI ($45,000), and stock issuance (20,000 × $28 = $560,000); subtract dividends ($100,000). Ending equity = $9,245,000. Option B ($9,200,000) is incorrect because it omits the $45,000 OCI component flowing through AOCI.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Interest paid classified as operating under U.S. GAAP
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Harlow Corp. paid $120,000 in interest on its outstanding bonds during 2025. The treasurer classifies this as a financing activity because it relates to borrowing. Under U.S. GAAP, is this classification correct?', '["Yes — interest paid relates to debt and is always classified as financing", "Yes — interest paid is classified as financing under both GAAP and IFRS", "No — interest paid is classified as investing because bonds fund asset purchases", "No — interest paid is classified as operating under U.S. GAAP"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-17, interest paid is classified as an operating activity under U.S. GAAP, regardless of the source of the underlying debt. Although interest relates to borrowing (a financing activity), the FASB concluded that interest payments affect net income determination and thus belong in operations. Option A is incorrect because under U.S. GAAP, interest paid is never classified as financing; IFRS allows that flexibility, but GAAP specifically requires operating classification.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Deferred tax liabilities always noncurrent under ASU 2015-17
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Lexington Corp. reports a deferred tax liability of $340,000, of which $120,000 relates to temporary differences reversing within 12 months and $220,000 relates to long-term differences. Under ASU 2015-17, how should the deferred tax liability be classified on the balance sheet?', '["$120,000 as current and $220,000 as noncurrent", "The entire $340,000 as a current liability", "The entire $340,000 as a noncurrent liability", "$120,000 as current and $220,000 netted against deferred tax assets"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ASU 2015-17 (codified in ASC 740-10-45-4) requires all deferred tax assets and liabilities to be classified as noncurrent, regardless of when the underlying temporary differences are expected to reverse. The previous requirement to bifurcate deferred taxes into current and noncurrent portions was eliminated. Option A is incorrect because it reflects the pre-ASU treatment, which classified deferred taxes based on the reversal period of each temporary difference.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Operating cycle definition
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'In the context of current versus noncurrent classification, what is the operating cycle?', '["The time from the purchase of inventory to the collection of cash from its sale", "The period between two consecutive balance sheet dates", "The average number of days to convert cash into investments", "The time required to prepare and issue financial statements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 210-10-45-3, the operating cycle is the time it takes for a business to convert cash to inventory, sell the inventory, and collect cash from customers. The operating cycle determines the current/noncurrent classification threshold when it exceeds one year. Option B is incorrect because it describes the fiscal period, not the operating cycle; the operating cycle measures the cash-to-cash conversion timeline of core operations.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Supplemental CF disclosures required for interest and taxes paid
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Bridgewater Corp. uses the indirect method for its statement of cash flows. During 2025, the company paid $95,000 in interest on its bonds and $210,000 in income taxes. The controller asks whether these amounts must be separately disclosed. Under U.S. GAAP, what is required?', '["Neither amount requires separate disclosure under the indirect method", "Only income taxes paid must be disclosed; interest paid disclosure is optional", "Both interest paid and income taxes paid must be disclosed either on the face of the statement or in the notes", "Both amounts are automatically visible in the operating section and need no additional disclosure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 230-10-50-1 and 50-2, entities using the indirect method must separately disclose the amounts of interest paid (net of amounts capitalized) and income taxes paid during the period. These may appear on the face of the statement or in notes. Option D is incorrect because the indirect method starts with net income and makes aggregate adjustments, so individual cash payments for interest and taxes are not visible without supplemental disclosure.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Cash flow statement reconciliation: compute ending cash balance
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Chandler Corp. reports beginning cash of $180,000. Cash provided by operating activities is $420,000, cash used in investing activities is $275,000, and cash used in financing activities is $130,000. There is also a $15,000 effect of exchange rate changes on cash. What is the ending cash balance?', '["$195,000", "$325,000", "$180,000", "$210,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-24, the statement of cash flows reconciles beginning cash to ending cash by summing operating, investing, and financing cash flows, plus the effect of exchange rate changes. Beginning cash $180,000 + operating $420,000 − investing $275,000 − financing $130,000 + FX effect $15,000 = $210,000. Option A ($195,000) is incorrect because it omits the exchange rate effect, which is a required separate reconciling item on the cash flow statement.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — PUFER mnemonic application: realized gain NOT in OCI
(2, 'Financial Statements', 'Waverly Corp.''s controller uses the PUFER mnemonic to verify that all OCI components are properly reported. The company recorded pension prior service cost of $25,000, unrealized gain on AFS debt securities of $40,000, foreign currency translation loss of $10,000, and a realized gain on sale of equipment of $55,000. Applying the PUFER framework, which item does NOT belong in OCI?', '["Pension prior service cost of $25,000", "Unrealized gain on AFS debt securities of $40,000", "Foreign currency translation loss of $10,000", "Realized gain on sale of equipment of $55,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 220-10-45, the PUFER mnemonic identifies five OCI components: Pensions/OPEB, Unrealized gains on AFS debt, Foreign currency translation, Effective portion of cash flow hedges, and Risk (credit) changes on FV option liabilities. A realized gain on equipment sale is recognized in net income, not OCI, because it is a completed transaction. Option A is incorrect because pension adjustments are the "P" in PUFER and properly belong in OCI.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
